WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.387
Reading a model card.

2
00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:15.811
Hugging Face’s docs say a model card should describe the model, its intended uses and limitations (including biases and ethical considerations), the training parameters, the datasets used, and the evaluation results.

3
00:00:16.091 --> 00:00:29.488
The idea comes from “Model Cards for Model Reporting” by Mitchell and colleagues (2019), which proposed short documents that travel with a model and report how it performs for different groups and conditions.

4
00:00:29.768 --> 00:00:32.541
In practice, cards range from thorough to empty.

5
00:00:32.821 --> 00:00:34.613
Read five things, in this order.

6
00:00:34.893 --> 00:00:36.941
Intended use and out-of-scope use.

7
00:00:37.221 --> 00:00:40.485
This tells you what the publisher designed and tested for.

8
00:00:40.765 --> 00:00:47.891
A model “intended for research” or “not for medical use” is a warning you need to carry into your own risk review.

9
00:00:48.171 --> 00:00:49.216
Training data.

10
00:00:49.496 --> 00:00:54.829
Look for languages, the knowledge cutoff, and whether the data is named or only described.

11
00:00:55.109 --> 00:00:59.781
If the card says nothing, you can’t rule out that your test set was in the training data.

12
00:01:00.061 --> 00:01:01.149
Evaluations.

13
00:01:01.429 --> 00:01:07.275
Note which benchmarks, which settings (few-shot or zero-shot, with or without reasoning), and who ran them.

14
00:01:07.555 --> 00:01:09.155
These are the publisher’s claims.

15
00:01:09.435 --> 00:01:13.211
They help you shortlist; they don’t tell you how the model does on your task.

16
00:01:13.491 --> 00:01:14.387
License.

17
00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:19.125
Read the metadata field, then the license text itself, as in lesson 1.

18
00:01:19.405 --> 00:01:20.621
Usage details.

19
00:01:20.901 --> 00:01:26.875
The recommended generation settings, the chat template, the context length, and any special prompt format.

20
00:01:27.155 --> 00:01:31.741
Ignoring these is a common cause of “this model is worse than its benchmarks.

21
00:01:32.021 --> 00:01:32.319
”.
